## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design scalable, isolated QoS for multi-tenant CNFs with real-time config updates.

## Key Concepts
- Hierarchical token bucket, per-tenant and per-path
- Dynamic priority scheduling and cross-tenant isolation
- Fast policy propagation and SLA metrics
- Kubernetes manifests and minimal tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo token bucket checker
class TB {
  constructor(cap, ref) { this.cap = cap; this.refill = ref; }
  consume(n=1){ if(this.cap>=n){ this.cap-=n; return true;} return false; }
  refillAt(now){ this.cap = Math.min(this.max, this.cap + (now-this.last)*this.fillRate); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test SLA adherence under bursty traffic?
- How would you roll out policy changes safely across clusters?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical integration of image provenance with runtime attestation, ensuring defense in depth for CNF deployments in multi-region clusters.

## Key Concepts
- Image signing with Cosign and KMS-backed keys
- SPDX SBOM association and SBOM validation
- In-cluster attestation service generating verifiable tokens
- Admission control enforcing both provenance and attestation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: basic attestation gate
function attest(imageDigest, token, sbom) {
  if (!verifySignature(imageDigest)) return false;
  if (!verifySBOM(sbom)) return false;
  if (!verifyToken(token)) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle key rotation and revocation in the KMS?
- How would you simulate attestation failures in a namespace to verify policy behavior?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design tenant-aware rate limiting and observability in CNF gateways, a realistic production concern for multi-tenant platforms used by large companies.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-scoped policy API (REST)
- Sidecar token bucket limiter with TTL cache
- Real-time policy fetch and rotation
- Central audit/log sink and schema

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, fillRate) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.fillRate = fillRate;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }
  allow(n = 1) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.lastRefill) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.fillRate);
    this.lastRefill = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle policy drift during high load?
- What approaches ensure audit integrity and privacy?
